{"war":"paraguayan-war","start":"1864-11-12","end":"1870-03-01","note":"Drawn on 1864 borders throughout; Paraguayan invasions and the allied advance into Paraguay are occupied areas. Areas and arrows are approximate reconstructions from campaign maps and the cited accounts; the interior of Paraguay in 1869–70 is especially uncertain.","chapters":[{"id":"mato-grosso","title":"The invasion of Mato Grosso","start":"1864-11-12","end":"1865-04-30","bbox":[-59.0,-23.8,-53.8,-15.3],"arrows":["Paraguayan flotilla carries Barrios's column up the river to Fort Coimbra (December 1864)","Overland column to Nioaque and Miranda (December 1864 – January 1865)","Paraguayans take Albuquerque and Corumbá (January 1865)","Paraguayan advance to Coxim (April 1865)"],"text":"On 12 November 1864 Paraguay seized a Brazilian steamer near Concepción and broke off relations with Brazil, which had invaded Uruguay. In late December two Paraguayan columns invaded the Brazilian province of Mato Grosso: one went up the river and took Fort Coimbra and Corumbá, the other took Miranda and later Coxim. The Paraguayans did not push on to Cuiabá, the provincial capital, and held the south of the province until 1868."},{"id":"corrientes","title":"Corrientes and Riachuelo","start":"1865-04-12","end":"1865-10-21","bbox":[-60.0,-30.3,-57.8,-27.0],"arrows":["Paraguayan squadron and Robles's army seize Corrientes (13 April 1865)","Robles advances south along the Paraná to Goya (April 1865)"],"text":"Refused passage across Argentine territory, Paraguay seized the city of Corrientes on 13 April 1865, and General Robles's army advanced down the Paraná to Goya. On 11 June the Brazilian fleet destroyed Paraguay's squadron at Riachuelo, below Corrientes, and the allies controlled the rivers from then on. With its supply lines cut, the occupation became useless, and on 3 October López ordered his southern army home."},{"id":"uruguay-river","title":"The Uruguay River and Uruguaiana","start":"1865-05-20","end":"1865-09-18","bbox":[-57.8,-30.2,-55.3,-27.2],"arrows":["Estigarribia's column marches down the Uruguay River (May – August 1865)","Duarte's column on the Argentine bank to Paso de los Libres (June – August 1865)","Flores's allied army attacks Duarte at Yatay (17 August 1865)"],"text":"A second Paraguayan army under Colonel Estigarribia marched down the Uruguay River, taking São Borja and Itaqui in Brazil, while Major Duarte's column moved along the Argentine bank. On 17 August Flores's allied army destroyed Duarte's column at Yatay. Estigarribia, besieged in Uruguaiana, surrendered with about 5,500 men on 18 September 1865."},{"id":"tuyuti","title":"Into Paraguay: Tuyutí and Curupayty","start":"1866-04-15","end":"1866-09-30","bbox":[-58.85,-27.45,-58.3,-26.95],"arrows":["The allies cross the Paraná into Paraguay (16 April 1866)","Allied advance to Tuyutí (April – May 1866)","Allied attack on Curuzú (3 September 1866)","Failed allied assault on Curupayty (22 September 1866)"],"text":"On 16 April 1866 the allies crossed the Paraná into Paraguay and advanced into the swamps before the fortress of Humaitá. López's great attack on their camp at Tuyutí on 24 May failed with terrible losses. The allies took Curuzú on 3 September, but their frontal assault on the trenches of Curupayty on 22 September was a bloody failure that halted them for ten months."},{"id":"humaita","title":"The siege of Humaitá","start":"1867-07-22","end":"1868-08-05","bbox":[-58.85,-27.45,-58.2,-26.75],"arrows":["Caxias's flanking march round Humaitá to Tayí (July – November 1867)","Brazilian ironclads force the passage of Humaitá (19 February 1868)"],"text":"From July 1867 the Marquess of Caxias marched the allied army round Humaitá, and on 2 November it reached Tayí on the river north of the fortress, cutting it off by land. On 19 February 1868 Brazilian ironclads ran past its guns, and López left with most of the garrison. The last defenders slipped out on the night of 24–25 July 1868, and the allies occupied the empty fortress on 26 July."},{"id":"dezembrada","title":"The Dezembrada and the fall of Asunción","start":"1868-07-26","end":"1869-01-05","bbox":[-58.85,-27.45,-56.8,-25.1],"arrows":["Allied advance north to Palmas (August – October 1868)","Caxias's road through the Chaco behind the Pikysyry line (November – December 1868)","The Dezembrada: Ytororó and Avay (6–11 December 1868)","Assault on Lomas Valentinas (21–27 December 1868)","Allied troops march into Asunción (1 January 1869)"],"text":"The allies advanced north along the river to the Pikysyry stream, where López had entrenched. Caxias built a road through the Chaco, recrossed the river behind the Paraguayan line and in December 1868 attacked from the rear, winning at Ytororó, Avay and Lomas Valentinas. Angostura surrendered on 30 December, and Brazilian troops entered Asunción on 1 January 1869."},{"id":"hills","title":"The Campaign of the Hills","start":"1869-08-01","end":"1869-08-19","bbox":[-57.8,-25.8,-56.4,-24.7],"arrows":["The Count of Eu's offensive into the Cordillera (August 1869)","Advance to Acosta Ñu and Caraguatay (August 1869)"],"text":"López raised a new army in the hills east of Asunción. In August 1869 the Count of Eu attacked: on 12 August his troops stormed Piribebuy, López's temporary capital, and on 16 August they crushed the Paraguayan rearguard, most of them boys, at Acosta Ñu. López fled north with the survivors."},{"id":"cerro-cora","title":"The pursuit to Cerro Corá","start":"1869-08-20","end":"1870-03-01","bbox":[-58.6,-25.5,-53.6,-21.5],"arrows":["Pursuit of López to Curuguaty (August – September 1869)","López's retreat to Cerro Corá (September 1869 – February 1870)","Câmara's column from Bela Vista to Cerro Corá (February – March 1870)"],"text":"Allied columns pursued López through the forests of the north-east, taking Curuguaty on 28 September 1869, while a Brazilian force based at Concepción guarded the river. In February 1870 López camped at Cerro Corá with a few hundred followers. On 1 March General Câmara's troops surprised the camp and killed him, ending the war."}],"fronts":[],"occupations":[{"name":"Paraguayan-occupied southern Mato Grosso (1864–1868)","side":1,"end":"1868-04-30","keys":[{"date":"1864-12-30","coords":[[-57.95,-21.9],[-58.0,-20.6],[-57.95,-19.95],[-57.7,-19.8],[-57.45,-20.3],[-57.1,-21.0],[-56.6,-21.9]]},{"date":"1865-01-20","coords":[[-57.95,-21.9],[-58.0,-20.6],[-57.95,-19.95],[-57.85,-19.3],[-57.8,-18.85],[-57.45,-18.8],[-57.05,-19.4],[-56.4,-19.85],[-55.85,-20.3],[-55.5,-21.0],[-55.35,-21.9]]},{"date":"1865-04-30","coords":[[-57.95,-21.9],[-58.0,-20.6],[-57.95,-19.95],[-57.85,-19.3],[-57.8,-18.85],[-57.45,-18.65],[-56.6,-18.45],[-55.4,-18.25],[-54.55,-18.25],[-54.45,-18.75],[-54.9,-19.6],[-55.35,-20.5],[-55.25,-21.9]]}]},{"name":"Paraguayan-occupied Corrientes (1865)","side":1,"end":"1865-11-03","keys":[{"date":"1865-04-13","coords":[[-58.4,-27.3],[-58.75,-27.36],[-58.92,-27.42],[-58.9,-27.58],[-58.7,-27.58],[-58.45,-27.48]]},{"date":"1865-04-30","coords":[[-58.3,-27.25],[-58.75,-27.36],[-58.95,-27.42],[-58.88,-27.95],[-59.12,-28.45],[-59.32,-29.1],[-59.15,-29.28],[-58.85,-28.62],[-58.58,-27.95],[-58.35,-27.6],[-58.05,-27.45]]},{"date":"1865-10-03","coords":[[-58.3,-27.25],[-58.75,-27.36],[-58.95,-27.42],[-58.88,-27.95],[-59.12,-28.45],[-59.32,-29.1],[-59.15,-29.28],[-58.85,-28.62],[-58.58,-27.95],[-58.35,-27.6],[-58.05,-27.45]]},{"date":"1865-10-22","coords":[[-58.3,-27.25],[-58.7,-27.33],[-58.72,-27.45],[-58.55,-27.55],[-58.3,-27.55],[-58.05,-27.45]]},{"date":"1865-11-02","coords":[[-58.45,-27.28],[-58.62,-27.32],[-58.6,-27.4],[-58.45,-27.52]]}]},{"name":"Paraguayan columns on the Uruguay River (1865)","side":1,"end":"1865-09-18","keys":[{"date":"1865-05-20","coords":[[-56.1,-27.4],[-55.7,-27.4],[-55.7,-27.9],[-55.85,-28.45],[-56.05,-28.62],[-56.2,-28.5],[-56.2,-27.9]]},{"date":"1865-06-12","coords":[[-56.1,-27.4],[-55.7,-27.4],[-55.65,-27.9],[-55.8,-28.45],[-55.88,-28.75],[-56.05,-28.78],[-56.22,-28.5